PSPATTERN 0.35.0a

    O on an empty cell repeats the last chain, phrase or instrument;
        O twice, quickly, makes a new one -- the next unused chain on
        the song screen, the next unused phrase on the chain screen,
        and on the phrase screen's instrument column a new instrument,
        a copy of the one you were using, in the next empty slot. The
        notice line says whether you got a repeat or a new one. The
        second tap only ever replaces what the first tap wrote.

PSPATTERN 0.34.0a

    The song's rows can be inserted and deleted, on TRIANGLE. Held
        with up or down it inserts a blank row above or below the
        cursor, or above or below a selection, and every column from
        that row moves down together, bookmarks with them. Held with
        X it deletes the cursor row after a yes, and everything below
        moves up. Both are refused while the song plays, an insert is
        refused when row FF holds anything, and both undo with R+L.
        Cut and paste stay in place: these are the only edits on the
        screen that move rows.

PSPATTERN 0.33.0a

    A sixth synth engine: hive, the chord engine. Up to five wavetable
        voices from one note -- a chord shape from a triad to a ninth,
        or off for a single note, spread to detune the voices against
        each other, width to place them across the stereo field. The
        wave is the tone list, saw, square, triangle or sine, from
        band-limited tables built once at start. Filter, envelopes,
        glide, drive and the LFO are shared by the whole chord. Every
        voice is one oscillator, so the voices row is the cost knob;
        the CPU LOAD bench has a hive row.

    CHRD on a hive note is a real chord: the nibbles are the
        intervals, every note sounds at once. Everywhere else it is
        the tick-rate flutter it always was. CHRD 0000 hands a hive
        note back to its chord row.

    Four hive presets: HIVE-PAD, HIVE-STAB, HIVE-SUPERSAW, HIVE-CHIME.
        The song screen's type box has an hv pill.

    The manual and the site now say that FM feedback belongs to
        operator four alone -- it does nothing while operator four's
        level is zero -- and that its bottom half is quiet by design.

PSPATTERN 0.32.0a

    A render no longer ends on the last note. The file stays open
        while the releases and the delay and reverb ring out, and
        closes on its own once the mix has been all but silent for a
        second and a half, or at fifteen seconds for a frozen reverb.
        A new note or a new take ends it at once. The screen says
        "letting the tail ring..." and then names the file it wrote.

    The card write has left the audio thread. A render used to hand
        each 32 KB lump to the Memory Stick from inside the render
        and wait for it; a card stalling on its own housekeeping was
        an underrun with a hiccup in it. The render now fills a ring
        and a thread of its own drains it to the card.

    Every take is its own file, named after the song and the clock:
        PIXELFALL-09042026-111433.wav, month, day, year, hour, minute,
        second. Renders never overwrite one another.

    START on the project screen renders. It read a stale copy of the
        render switch and never carried it to the mixer, so a render
        could only be started from the song screen.

PSPATTERN 0.31.1a

    The spectrum's first four bars moved as one because they were the
        same number: the log spacing over the analyser's 256 points put
        all of them on the first bin. No bar shares a bin with the one
        before it now, so the low bars move on their own. The analyser
        itself is unchanged -- a longer one was tried and starved the
        second core's send job, which the whole machine felt.

    A type box under the live box on the song screen: a pill for each
        kind of instrument -- sm sample, mi midi, tn tone, pd pdx, va
        vax, fm, vo vox -- lit in its own colour while something of
        that kind sounds on any channel.

PSPATTERN 0.31.0a

    A free-running LFO is a command. LFO_ aabb starts an LFO on the
        channel -- aa the rate, bb the depth, on the instrument's LFO
        destination, pitch if that is off -- that runs through rests
        and across notes and instruments until LFO_ 0000 stops it,
        another LFO_ re-tunes it without a hiccup, or the song stops.
        It needs no note under it: arm it on a bare step and whatever
        synth plays on that channel next carries it. Two channels
        started on one step at one rate stay locked. The lfo sync pill
        0.30.0a put on the instrument page is withdrawn: the
        instrument's own LFO restarts with every note, as it did
        before, and LFOD and LFOR still shape that one.

    LFO rates can be tempo divisions. F0 to FF -- on the instrument's
        rate, LFOR and LFO_ alike -- are cycles counted in ticks, eight
        bars down to a thirty-second with dotted and triplet values,
        exact at any tempo. A channel LFO on a division takes its
        phase from the song's own grid, so a bar-long sweep peaks on
        the downbeat wherever it was switched on. The table is in the
        manual under the command list.

    The free LFO measured its time in table ticks, which are twice as
        long on projects saved by older versions; it now counts audio
        slices, so it runs at the rate asked for on every project.

PSPATTERN 0.30.0a

    The command list opens on the phrase screen. O with up or down on
        a command column always had the code behind it, but the
        lookup still counted the columns the way they were before the
        velocity column pushed everything one to the right, so the
        picker found nothing and said nothing. It works, as the
        manual always said it did.

    VOLM slides on the synths. The rate in the first two digits only
        ever worked on samples; a synth jumped to the level whatever
        the rate said. It now walks there over the same time the
        sampler takes, rate times four ticks. Rate 00 still jumps.

    Synth LFOs can run free. A sync pill beside the LFO destination on
        every engine page: note restarts the LFO with each note, as it
        always did; free lets it carry on from wherever the last note
        on that channel left it, so a slow sweep keeps sweeping across
        a run of notes. Saved with the project and with presets; older
        songs and presets load as note.

PSPATTERN 0.29.0a

    Importing is two pages and one key. The browser is a full screen:
        the file list with a FILE panel beside it saying what the file
        under the cursor is. TRIANGLE chooses the file and opens its
        settings page -- stereo or folded to mono, rate as it is,
        halved or quartered, the memory cost following, and an IMPORT
        panel spelling out what will happen. TRIANGLE again imports;
        X goes back to the list with nothing taken. Nothing else
        imports. O never does, so the O-and-arrow editing habit
        changes a setting instead of taking a file, and a stray X
        cannot leave the screen from the settings page. Importing a
        name the project already holds asks first, with No selected.

    TRIANGLE is a button now. It does one thing, on one screen, so
        no habit from anywhere else can land on it. It is built in
        like SELECT, so a stale mapping.xml cannot leave it dead. The
        built-in defaults for O and X used to be square and triangle,
        a leftover nobody saw because mapping.xml always overrode it;
        they are circle and cross now, as the manual says.

    Hear it as it will be stored. SELECT on either page plays the file
        through the same fold and average the import writes, resampled
        to pitch, so a quarter-rate preview sounds exactly as the
        sample will. Change a setting while it plays and the sound
        follows. This fixed a defect on the way: the preview copied
        samples one for one, so a 22050Hz file played an octave up and
        a 48kHz file flat. Every preview is at pitch now.

    A samples screen, off a new row on the project screen. Every wav
        and soundfont the project carries, what each costs in memory
        and which instruments are on it, plus files on the card the
        pool could not load. SELECT hears one, O removes it after a
        yes/no that names who is on it. Removal is safe while a voice
        is still ringing: it is cut and the instrument set to no sample
        before the memory goes. Compact inst is the same thing done
        to every unused sample at once; this is the one to use to see
        what would go first.

    An instrument whose sample is removed simply has none until one is
        assigned again: its file row reads "none", the project saves it
        that way and loads it back the same. Assigning a sample to a
        slot whose name had failed to match at load used to keep the
        old name for the next save; it does not now.

PSPATTERN 0.28.0a

    Importing a sample asks how to store it. Choosing a wav in the
        browser opens a second step: the top says what the file is --
        channels, rate, word size, length, size on the card and what
        it costs in memory -- and two rows below choose the shape the
        project keeps: stereo or folded to mono, and the rate as it
        is, halved or quartered. An "in ram" line follows the choices.
        Mono halves what a stereo file costs and halving the rate
        halves it again; the sampler plays any rate at the right
        pitch, so a 22050Hz copy sounds the same note with less top
        end. The reduction is a plain average, not a filtered
        resample -- bright material can alias, which on this machine
        is as often wanted as not. The choices are remembered until
        you quit, so a batch at one setting is O, O per file. SELECT
        still plays the original. Soundfonts skip the step.

    The copy the project keeps is always 16-bit PCM, the form the
        sampler holds in memory. A 24-bit or float source used to be
        copied as it came and converted on every project open; now
        the folder holds only what the machine plays, and opens
        faster for it. A 16-bit file stored as it is comes across
        byte for byte.

    Importing a name the project already holds replaces that sample.
        Every instrument on it picks up the new version; their start,
        loop and end reset to the new length. The new file is written
        beside the old one and moved into place only when complete,
        so a full card cannot take the existing copy with it.

    The sample page says what the slot is working with. A FILE panel
        at the bottom shows the whole name -- the file row shows
        seventeen characters of it -- then channels, rate, length in
        seconds, length in frames (hex, the unit start, loop and end
        count in) and what it costs in memory. A generated kit drum
        says so; a soundfont preset says it carries a sample per note.

    There is no word size choice, on purpose: memory is always 16-bit,
        and the crush row already does bit reduction at play time.

PSPATTERN 0.27.1a

    Quasi-standby: the screen now stays truly dark. The backlight was
        creeping back on about 30 seconds into a rest -- that was the
        system's own backlight auto-off setting firing and taking
        control back from the manual blackout. Rest now suspends that
        system timer for its duration and drives the backlight
        directly (kernel), so it goes fully dark and stays dark until
        you wake it, then restores your setting. Wake relights the
        screen instantly (no button-press needed).

PSPATTERN 0.27.0a

    Quasi-standby: the power switch now rests the machine instead of a
        true suspend. True standby is off the table while the second
        core (the Media Engine, running the send FX) is live -- Sony's
        suspend cannot safely re-initialise it -- so the switch is
        repurposed. Slide it and the project auto-saves, a screen
        explains this is a low-power REST (not a real standby, it keeps
        draining slowly) with a rough battery-life estimate, then a
        15-second countdown, then the screen goes truly dark and the
        clock drops to minimum. Slide again -- or press any button --
        and it wakes instantly, screen and audio back at once. A
        descending chirp marks the way down, an ascending one the way
        back. On a nearly-empty battery it beeps every 30s while
        resting, so a long rest never quietly dies with unsaved-feeling
        work (it was saved on the way down regardless). Saves roughly
        5-10x over leaving it on; note it is not a true standby and
        will not hold for days.

PSPATTERN 0.26.2a

    The freeze after standby is fixed, and it was the second core.
        Waking pulsed the ME's reset so it rebooted from its resident
        image: the image survives a sleep in main RAM, but the core's
        STACK lives in eDRAM, which standby wipes. A processor
        restarted onto a wiped stack runs unknown code with write
        access to everything, and its damage surfaced wherever the
        program next happened to touch -- the project teardown one
        run, leaving the picker the next, never twice the same. That
        wandering is what finally named it: deadlocks do not move,
        corruption does.

        The core is now held in reset from the wake onwards and
        nothing revives it. Every render takes the scalar fallback,
        which is bit-identical: after a standby the dsp figure runs
        higher, the me meter reads 0 and the spectrum stops until the
        program is next started. A slower machine beats an
        unpredictable one. Reviving it properly -- re-uploading the
        image at wake instead of trusting residency -- is the follow
        up, and worth doing with a dependable baseline underneath.

    The audio device is opened once and kept for the run. Every
        project load used to close and reopen it, and SDL's close
        waits for its own audio thread to come out of a driver that a
        standby had taken the channel from. Nothing needed the churn:
        buffer size and prebuffer are reboot settings already.

    The render thread is waited for rather than hoped about: stopping
        it posted a request, slept ten milliseconds and deleted the
        thread object, which a single render block can outlive.

PSPATTERN 0.26.1a (tester build)

    The memory stick is woken with everything else. Resume restored
        the second core, the controller, the screen, the audio and
        the USB link, and assumed the card would come back by itself.
        It does not, reliably -- and the first thing anyone does on
        waking is load a project, which is nothing but card reads.
        That is the rare freeze after a standby, and after the
        battery cut the power. Resume now waits for the card to
        answer before the program touches it, up to two seconds, then
        carries on either way.

    A damaged recovery file says so. The autosave is written by a
        machine that may lose power mid-write, which is what it is
        for, so a truncated one is expected: it was refused safely
        but silently, leaving a half-restored project and no
        explanation. It now says "recovery file damaged".

PSPATTERN 0.26.0a

    No groove index can walk off the array. The groove table is 32
        rows of 16 bytes and every read indexed it raw -- including
        with 255, which the code uses as a legal sentinel for the
        table's default groove and which lands four kilobytes past
        the array. The screen edits through the same unchecked
        pointer, so an out of range cursor wrote there as well: the
        profile of a machine that powers itself off while you are
        looking at a screen. Every access is bounds checked now.

    A new project starts with a clean mixer. The mixer and the groove
        table are singletons that outlive a project, and restore only
        overwrites what a file actually contains -- so every setting
        a project did not mention arrived inherited from the song
        before it. Both are cleared before the restore now. (Songs
        saved on earlier builds may have written a few inherited
        values as their own; worth a glance at the mixer on older
        projects.)

    O on the phrase screen's note column auditions on every press
        again, and the note is remembered as last touched again, so
        the click-a-note-then-click-an-empty-cell habit works.

PSPATTERN 0.25.9a (tester build)

    No groove index can walk off the array. The groove table is 32
        rows of 16 bytes and every read indexed it raw -- including
        with 255, which the code uses as a legal sentinel for the
        table's default groove and which lands four kilobytes past
        the array. The screen edits through the same unchecked
        pointer, so an out of range cursor wrote there too: the
        profile of a machine that powers itself off. Every access is
        bounds checked now, the setters clamp both ends, and the
        mixer column that stands in for the song cursor is clamped
        low as well as high.

PSPATTERN 0.25.8a (tester build)

    A new project starts with a clean mixer. The mixer and the groove
        table are singletons that outlive a project: they cleared once
        at boot and then carried whatever the last song put in them,
        and restore only overwrites what a file actually contains --
        so every setting a project did not mention arrived inherited
        from the song before it. Both are cleared before the restore
        now. (Songs saved on earlier builds may have written a few
        inherited values as their own; worth a glance at the mixer on
        older projects.)

    O on the note column auditions every press again. Audition mode
        keeps running silently after its note ends, and the preview
        only fired when the player was not running -- so the first
        press played the row and every one after did nothing.

    The last-touched note is remembered again. The second-press
        gesture, armed by O on a TBL parameter, survived a cursor
        move and swallowed the next O on the note column -- taking
        the audition and the copy/paste habit with it.

PSPATTERN 0.25.7a

    Every screen jumps through the same gate. The navigation rules
        covered the screens with their own drill logic and let the
        rest slide through: from the mixer, config, FX, groove or the
        tables, a jump landed on whatever was last open whether or
        not anything was placed, and from the chain screen the table
        tiles neither refused nor greyed. One generic gate now: the
        song cursor's context (the mixer's column standing in for its
        cursor), the same refusals and notifications at every level,
        stopping at the deepest screen that still makes sense -- and
        the map greys by the same rule, so they cannot disagree.

PSPATTERN 0.25.6a

    The screen map greys what you cannot reach yet. A destination the
        drill would refuse -- an empty chain row below the song
        cursor, an instrument with no table set, a phrase row with no
        TBL and no instrument table -- draws dimmed on the R menu, so
        the map says what is reachable before you try. It follows the
        cursor live, and the greying can never disagree with the
        refusals because both ask the same logic. Committing onto a
        dimmed tile still gets the refusal and its reason.

PSPATTERN 0.25.5a

    Navigation tells the truth, the LSDJ way. A jump with nothing to
        follow is refused where you stand, with a reason: an empty
        chain row does not lead to the phrase screen ("empty row -
        place a phrase first"), an instrument with no table does not
        lead to the table screen, a phrase row with no TBL and no
        instrument table does not either. Deep jumps stop at the
        deepest screen that still makes sense. The one kept
        convenience: an empty song slot still makes its chain on the
        way in.

    A new chain is born empty, and so is a new phrase. Allocation
        used to hand back whatever the number held in a previous
        life, so fresh slots arrived haunted by old parts.

    START on the phrase and chain screens plays what the screen
        shows -- the viewed phrase, looping, or the viewed chain --
        instead of resolving through the song grid and coming up
        silent when the cursor context did not line up. SELECT+START
        plays the full song from either screen, the LSDJ chord.

PSPATTERN 0.25.4a

    The thumbstick is retired. It only ever spoke on the instrument
        screen, and stick drift -- near universal on aging PSPs --
        made that screen a lottery for affected units: focus walking
        and values editing themselves. No clamp truly tames a pad
        drifting past its thresholds, so nothing listens to the
        stick any more. The d-pad does everything it did: two zones,
        X+up/down between them, up/down and left/right within the
        selector rows, O with a direction to edit a parameter.

PSPATTERN 0.25.3a

    The dying d-pad is cured. Lose one button-release event -- a
        momentarily full event queue is enough -- and the program
        believed that button was held forever: a phantom SELECT sent
        every arrow into the bookmark branch, a phantom R into the
        screen map, a phantom O into value edits. Controls dead for
        seconds while the song played on, on any screen, until the
        stuck button happened to be pressed again. The held-button
        state is now checked against the pad itself, one button at a
        time, and a phantom is dropped after ~190ms of the hardware
        saying "up" -- no synthetic events, nothing a real chord can
        trip over. All ten buttons covered.

    Cycling an effect type no longer replays the row; preview-on-
        press belongs to the note column.

    START works on the FX screen.

    The table screen follows what sent you there, from the instrument
        screen and the phrase screen alike, on both map tiles.

PSPATTERN 0.25.2a

    O on the instrument column is plain SET. An invisible second-press
        gesture used to replace an established instrument with a
        freshly allocated blank one -- the note kept sounding through
        a different, empty sound, and nothing said why. Empty cell
        takes the last instrument, full cell becomes the last, and
        that is all. Pressing O there no longer auditions the row
        either; preview-on-press belongs to the note column.

    The phrase-to-table jump resolves like playback: a TBL command on
        the cursor's row wins, else the governing instrument's table
        -- the instrument on the row or the nearest one above, the
        same search playback uses -- else the screen stays put.

PSPATTERN 0.25.1a

    The table jump follows from both map tiles. The table editor owns
        two cells of the screen map, and the follow-the-instrument
        coupling checked only the first -- arrive on the other and
        the jump showed whatever table was last open. Both couple
        now, from the instrument screen (its own table row), the
        phrase screen (the TBL command under the cursor, else the
        row's instrument), and the deep jump from the song screen.

PSPATTERN 0.25.0a

    The stability release. A week of tester sessions traced every
        freeze to its mechanism, and each one is closed:

    Editing no longer stalls. The autosave backstop fired mid-
        keypress after two minutes of steady work -- the freeze, and
        the dead direction afterwards, as the button release was
        swallowed. It waits for a half-second gap now.

    Compacting a project is safe. The purge walked through the
        generated kit's slots and the next reload freed shifted
        memory twice: heap corruption, a load frozen on its first
        file, cured only by reboot. The purge starts above the kit.

    Sample audition works, and its stray write is gone: the filter
        bank was sized for eight channels while the preview plays on
        a ninth.

    A wav header can no longer size the heap, a truncated file can no
        longer hang the loader, and a failed sample fails alone
        instead of taking the machine with it.

    Cut clears and paste overwrites on every screen, LSDJ-style. The
        song grid no longer splices rows. O on an empty song slot
        makes a NEW chain, and drilling into an empty slot creates
        that slot's chain instead of showing a stale one.

    The table screen follows what sent you: the instrument's own
        table from the instrument screen, the TBL command under the
        cursor from the phrase screen.

    Importing is a menu item -- "import sample >" under the type row,
        one press, samplelib created if missing. Previews play the
        file you asked for, when you asked, without the click, and
        stop when you let go. One sample list: kit first, imports
        after.

    START works on the FX screen. Cycling an effect type no longer
        replays the row. The info panel shows memory USED -- the one
        figure this machine can report honestly -- and LOG=YES writes
        a log that survives the session.

PSPATTERN 0.24.7a (tester build)

    Compacting a project no longer corrupts the session. The purge
        walked through the generated drum kit's slots, deleting pool
        entries that were never on the card and shifting everything
        under the pool's feet -- the next reload freed the same
        memory twice, and the load after that froze on the first
        file it touched. Only a reboot cleared it. The purge starts
        above the kit now, and a truncated file or a soured
        directory read can no longer hang the loader either.

    One sample list. The KIT/WAV engine split is withdrawn: the
        generated kit sits at the top of the list, imports follow.
        "import sample >" keeps its row under the type row; the file
        row steps through sounds and never opens the browser.

    START works on the FX screen -- the place you stand while A/B-ing
        an effect against the playing song.

    Cycling an effect type in the phrase screen no longer replays the
        row on every step. Auto-preview belongs to note, instrument
        and velocity only.

    The log, when LOG=YES, actually survives: every line lands on the
        card as written instead of evaporating with the session.

PSPATTERN 0.24.6a (tester build)

    The stall while editing is gone. Every guard holding the autosave
        off ended with "unless we have been dirty for two minutes",
        and editing steadily for two minutes is what working on a
        song is -- so the write landed in the middle of a keypress:
        the machine stopped for a second or more, and the button
        release that arrived during the stall was lost, leaving that
        direction dead until it was pressed again. The backstop now
        waits for half a second with no button pressed.

    One press, one chain. O on an empty song slot makes a new chain,
        and pressing it again no longer hands you a different one and
        abandons the first.

    Going into an empty song slot makes that slot's chain instead of
        showing whichever chain was last looked at.

    After importing a sample the cursor lands on the file row.

PSPATTERN 0.24.5a (tester build)

    A new song slot gets a chain of its own. O on an empty slot used
        to put back the last chain you touched, so two song positions
        could hold the same chain without saying so -- and a phrase
        written at one quietly rewrote the other. It makes a new
        chain now. Going into an empty slot makes one too, instead of
        showing whatever chain was last looked at.

    The info panel says "used", not "mem". Free memory cannot be had
        on this machine: every way of asking returns a number that
        never moves, each wrong in its own direction. What the
        allocator has handed out is exact and moves with every sample
        loaded, so that is what the panel shows.

    The sample memory work of the last builds is withdrawn. On-demand
        loading and its memory gate refused samples in songs that fit
        with room to spare, because the figure they trusted was a
        constant. Songs load as they did before.

PSPATTERN 0.24.4a (tester build)

    Sample audition works again, and a stray write is gone with it.
        The sampler's filter bank was sized for eight channels while
        the preview plays on a ninth, so auditioning a sample wrote
        its filter state past the end of that array: silence from the
        preview, and a stray write into whatever followed.

    A missed button release no longer blinds the navigation. The
        screen map opens only when nothing else is held, so one lost
        release meant it stopped appearing at all and every gesture
        read as a chord. The held-button set is now checked against
        the hardware and heals itself within a frame.

    KIT and WAV are two engines. A sample instrument picks its engine
        under the type row exactly like a synth does: KIT plays the
        generated drum kit, WAV plays what you imported. Importing
        belongs to WAV, and an empty WAV engine is a place you can
        stand -- that is where the import row lives.

    Cut clears, paste overwrites, on every screen. The song grid used
        to splice: cut pulled the whole song up, paste pushed it
        down. The chain, phrase and table screens never did that, and
        neither does LSDJ or M8. Now nothing moves but what is under
        the cursor.

    The mem figure moves when memory does. It was probing for the
        largest allocatable block, which on this machine parks at its
        own ceiling; it asks the allocator now.

    Instrument screen: X+up/down crosses between the selector rows
        and the parameters, and the d-pad walks whichever it is in.

PSPATTERN 0.24.3a (tester build)

    A wav header can no longer size the heap. The data chunk states
        its own length, and that number decided both the allocation
        and how much got written into it -- with nothing checking it
        against the file. A big or corrupt claim wrapped the maths
        and wrote past the buffer: heap corruption, which is what
        freezes with no pattern and a machine too sick to draw the
        XMB actually look like. The claim is clamped to the file now,
        the maths is 64-bit, and short reads report failure instead
        of turning stale memory into audio.

    A sample reporting no frames can no longer wedge the machine. Its
        loop end came out at -1 and the render walked it forever, on
        the audio thread.

    Importing is a menu item. "import sample >" sits under the type
        row; the browser opens on one press, and samplelib is created
        if it is missing.

    The sample list has two banks. KIT is the generated drum kit, WAV
        is what you imported -- the file row walks one half, not
        eighty entries.

    Previews behave. SELECT plays the file under the cursor and stops
        when you move or release; it no longer plays everything you
        browse past, no longer starts one press late, and no longer
        clicks at each end.

    Instrument screen navigation: X+up/down crosses between the
        selector rows and the parameters, and the d-pad walks
        whichever it is in.

PSPATTERN 0.24.2a (tester build)

    The runaway d-pad is fixed. A drifting analog stick could flood
        the input queue until real button releases were dropped: the
        cursor slid hard one way and pinned, input froze, and the
        screen sometimes jumped to the mixer on its own. Drift now
        fires at most once and stays quiet, and the stick only speaks
        at all on the instrument screen, the one place that listens.

    Audition has a channel of its own. The preview no longer borrows
        the song cursor's column, so no mute, fader, filter or send
        can silence or colour it -- SELECT always plays the
        instrument raw, at full volume.

PSPATTERN 0.24.1a

    Save-as saves. It never actually worked on the PSP: the flow
        glued its destination path together by hand, the alias
        resolver added a slash of its own, and the memory stick
        driver refused the double-slashed result -- so pressing OK
        did nothing at all. Paths are now built the same way the
        boot-time new-project flow always built them. Save-as writes
        the project and its samples, then drops you straight into
        the new copy.

    The project screen stops haunting. Save-as used to open the
        project picker underneath the copy it was about to load,
        leaving an orphaned dialog with stale wiring -- phantom
        screens, saves that seemed to land only on the way out,
        freezes and crashes around the picker. Closed properly now.

    Exiting from the picker no longer crashes. Backing out of the
        project picker quits the app, and quitting never stopped the
        MIDI threads: the usb pump kept polling a driver that was
        being torn down underneath it. Shutdown now asks every MIDI
        thread out before the usb teardown -- this also makes the
        HOME exit clean with the adapter attached.

PSPATTERN 0.24.0a

    The thumbstick moves where you aim. The two stick axes used to
        fire independently, so a push at forty degrees moved the
        cursor two directions at once. Now one tick fires one axis:
        the clearly dominant one wins, and a push sitting right on a
        diagonal deliberately does nothing until the angle commits.

    (And from 0.23.1a, now public: the wav loader speaks every
        dialect -- EXTENSIBLE headers, odd metadata chunks, 24-bit,
        32-bit float -- proven by a generated-file test suite. If a
        wav refuses to load now, send the file; it is genuinely
        unusual.)

PSPATTERN 0.23.1a

    The sample loader speaks every WAV dialect. Files wrapped in the
        EXTENSIBLE header modern DAWs write, files with odd-sized
        metadata chunks, 24-bit exports, 32-bit float, 32-bit int:
        all of them load now, converted to 16-bit on the way in. If a
        wav refused to load before, drop it in again -- and if one
        still refuses, please send the file itself: it is now
        something genuinely unusual.

PSPATTERN 0.23.0a

    Leader mode earns its name. Outgoing MIDI clock used to leave at
        the moment a tick was DECIDED, quantised to audio fragments
        -- so downstream gear ran one audio pipeline early against
        what this machine was heard playing. A dedicated sender now
        schedules every clock, start and stop byte for the moment
        its beat is HEARD, sub-millisecond, at any tempo and any
        buffer setting. sendo on the config screen trims for gear
        that is slow to sound; zero suits nearly everything.

    Who owns the clock moved to where it belongs. The sync role is a
        property of the rig -- which cable goes where -- not of the
        song, so it is now mode in the config screen's SYNC panel:
        LEADER (the default), FOLLOW, or OFF. The per-song sync
        field is gone; old songs load untouched.

    The second processor's meter tells the truth at rest. It read a
        steady fifteen percent with the song stopped: a spectrum
        that kept computing silence, and a load reference measured
        on a bus quieter than the one the machine actually runs on.
        The spectrum now sleeps with the transport, idle is reported
        as the zero it provably is, and the reference recalibrates
        itself against real conditions.

    The exit option on the project screen is gone. Quitting is what
        the HOME button is for -- the road the system hardened --
        and the in-app path froze often enough that closing it beat
        maintaining it.

PSPATTERN 0.22.0a

    UPDATE BOTH FILES: this release changes usbmidi.prx for the first
        time in many versions. Copy the new EBOOT.PBP AND the new
        usbmidi.prx to the card together; an old prx still works but
        loses the sync improvement below.

    MIDI clock follow locks in phase, not just in tempo. The loop
        feeds on counts the USB pump cannot lose, smooths the bursts
        the pump cannot help, measures the leader's tempo inside the
        first beat, and the driver now stamps every clock byte with
        its kernel arrival time -- wire time, upstream of every
        thread wake. The tracker runs early by its own measured
        latency at any tempo and any buffer setting, so the synco
        trim (new on the config screen) describes only your gear:
        dial it once against your DAW -- around 50 for a computer
        over a USB interface -- and it holds through tempo changes,
        buffer changes and whole sets.

    Faster defaults, earned. The audio pipeline ships at 128 x 5
        (14.5ms, down from 35) because after the playback hardening
        a maxed-out song holds it with zero underruns -- and the und
        figure on the song screen referees the claim on your songs.
        64 is proven on a slim unit if you want the tightest feel.

PSPATTERN 0.21.0a

    The rock-solid release. A thirty-two-agent audit hunted every way
        playback could drop out, stall or die; seventeen confirmed
        findings, seventeen fixed. Among them: a tempo nudge that
        could corrupt memory inside the audio callback below 22 BPM;
        a lock-ordering flaw that could hang the machine for good if
        you browsed presets while a song played; the project save
        starving the audio for up to two seconds; and a sample
        command that could spin the render thread forever. Saving
        now asks the transport to stop first. Everything that
        touches the Memory Stick from the interface -- preset loads
        and saves, config writes, sample previews, the debug log --
        now runs where the audio thread never waits on it.

    The send bus grew a real safety margin: its wet ring is four
        times deeper, rebuilds its own cushion after a late block
        instead of staying wounded for the rest of the take, and
        crossing between the two processors (a suspend, a very slow
        tempo) starts the delay clean instead of replaying ghosts.

    A vfpu row in the info panel: the share of the render spent in
        vector code, so you can watch the coprocessor earn its keep.
        And the FM engine is selectable out of the box now -- the
        dsp figure shows its cost, which beats forbidding it.

PSPATTERN 0.20.0a

    The dead knobs live. duck, gate and comp on the FX screen have
        been sliders with nothing behind them since they shipped;
        now the send you feed the reverb ducks its tail, gates it
        for the drum trick, and a one-knob compressor glues the wet
        bus before the drive saturates it. Three new knobs join
        them: locut cuts the mud out of what enters the tail --
        freeze stops turning to sludge -- width spreads the reverb
        past the edges of the room, and tone filters inside the
        delay's feedback loop so every repeat dies a little darker,
        like tape. All of it runs on the second processor with an
        identical arithmetic mirror on the main one, and a delay
        knob at zero is bit-exact silence about its own existence.

    A spectrum of the mix, under the scope. The second processor
        computes it in its spare moments -- it never delays a note
        -- and the song screen draws it: bass left, air right,
        cleared the moment the song stops.

    Autosave learned manners. It already refused to write during
        playback; now it also waits for the OUTPUT to be silent, so
        a tail ringing past the end of a song is never chewed by
        the Memory Stick. And for the stage there is an asave
        switch on the config screen: set NO and the machine never
        touches the stick on its own, effective immediately.

    Below 54 BPM the send bus slowly starved -- jobs longer than the
        second processor's buffers were quietly truncated. Those
        tempos now take the single-core path, which has no such cap.

PSPATTERN 0.19.0a

    The song screen's mix panel becomes the info panel, and it now
        tells you how the machine itself is doing: the render load
        (quiet until a block actually misses its deadline, then the
        worst recent figure with an exclamation mark), how busy the
        second processor is with the send effects, the sample memory
        still free, and how many times the audio queue ran dry since
        play started. Underruns were never counted before; now the
        moment the driver serves silence, it shows.

    The second processor's load is measured without a clock on that
        core -- it has none worth the name. Its idle loop already
        beats a heartbeat; a job stops the beating, so the load is
        the drop in heartbeat rate against a rate calibrated once at
        boot. The two obvious clock sources were tried first: one
        does not tick on this silicon, and reading the other from
        that core killed it outright.

PSPATTERN 0.18.1a

    The preview is a key. SELECT plays the instrument for exactly as
        long as it is held: press for note on, release for note off.
        The latch, the retrigger tap and the half-second hold-to-stop
        are gone -- tap between preset steps and you are browsing the
        bank by ear, one held note at a time.

PSPATTERN 0.18.0a

    The SELECT button works. It was only ever wired through
        mapping.xml, so any card whose copy predated the line had a
        dead button and no way to know -- the instrument preview and
        the song bookmarks both. The mapping is now built in like
        every other button; no card file needs updating.

    Preset browsing is a browser now. Stepping the row loads silently;
        SELECT is what demos the sound. The "--" entry is a place you
        can come back to: the sound is snapshotted when browsing
        leaves it and restored when you step back, so surfing the
        bank can always be backed out of. X cancels the save-name
        dialog. And presets whose names came back from the card in
        uppercase -- FAT returns short names that way -- no longer
        show a stray extension and fail to load.

    The factory bank grows to a hundred and nine sounds. TONE gains a
        drum kit -- kick, snare, hat, zap -- and chip voices at exact
        NES duty cycles; PDX gains CZ brass, an 808 with glide and
        the warp-wobble growl; VAX covers the dance floor with a
        reese, rave stabs and a trance pluck; FM adds slap bass,
        tubular bells, a music box and a talkbox; VOX now sings in
        male and female choirs, growls, whistles and dials a modem.

PSPATTERN 0.17.0a

    Synth presets. The synth page grows a third rung on the identity
        ladder: type, engine, and now preset. Left and right step
        through the bank and stepping IS loading -- with the audition
        latched, surfing the row plays each sound as it lands, so you
        choose with your ears. Tap O on the row to save the current
        sound under a name of your own; it lands beside the factory
        set in presets/ next to the EBOOT, as plain name=value text
        you can read, edit or share.

    Fifty factory sounds ship in the folder, ten per engine, each
        designed against what its engine's code actually renders --
        noise hats and wind from TONE, a wah clav and a hard sync
        from PDX, an acid line and a ring-mod clang from VAX, a
        drawbar organ and a gamelan from FM, whispers and a Dalek
        from VOX. Loading a preset never drags a table along, and a
        preset that names an engine you have hidden still loads and
        plays -- choosing it by name is opting in.

PSPATTERN 0.16.0a

    Standby works. Slide the power switch and the machine sleeps;
        slide it again and the song, the screen and the second
        processor all come back. The Media Engine is why this was ever
        broken: its library replaces the firmware's own suspend
        handling, and with nothing in the replacement the core went
        down mid-job and the machine rebooted -- which is why the
        switch was locked out entirely. Now the core is held in reset
        while the machine sleeps and reboots from its resident image
        at wake, the controller and display re-arm on the way up, and
        if the wake ever goes wrong the effects simply fall back to
        the main processor rather than hanging anything. Verified on
        hardware through suspends mid-reverb and back-to-back cycles.

    And a navigation fix: jumping deep through the map in one gesture
        -- song straight to phrase, or all the way to the instrument
        screen -- now carries the cursor's context through every level
        it crosses, so the phrase you land on is the one reachable
        through the cell you were standing on, exactly as the two-hop
        route always did.

PSPATTERN 0.15.0a

    The instrument screen becomes an instrument. Each control owns one
        job: the d-pad stays on the identity ladder -- left and right
        step the type or the engine, up and down move between them, and
        it never wanders into the parameters. The analog nub, mapped to
        nothing since the day the hardware shipped, browses the
        parameters; hold O and it turns the focused value, ticking
        slowly under a gentle push so exact numbers are easy to land
        and speeding up with real deflection. L with the d-pad switches
        instrument from anywhere, X with the d-pad browses parameters
        for the day the nub wears out, and SELECT previews: every press
        plays the instrument, every type, engine or instrument step
        re-sounds it while armed, and holding it silences. Changing
        type loses nothing any more -- the settings of the type you
        leave are kept and restored when you return, and the "settings
        are lost" warning is gone because nothing is.

    The screen map became the navigation. Hold R and the map appears
        with your screen highlighted; the arrows walk the highlight
        over exactly the grid that is drawn, and releasing R jumps
        there. Every screen reachable from everywhere, one mental
        model, and the drills keep their context -- entering the chain
        from the song still follows the cursor.

    A hunt through every corner of the renderer, verified change by
        change. The synth engines stopped paying for a heap-allocating
        parameter lookup forty times a voice per block; the default
        master no longer defeats its own unity skip over a rounding
        error; a dead double pitch computation left the FM path; the
        wide-open VAX lowpass now skips its filter the way TONE does.
        That last one is audible: "open" used to roll the top octave
        off, and open now means open -- pull cutoff to around 230 to
        get the old shade back. Project saves fell from a two second
        freeze to a blink, sample imports run in sixty-four kilobyte
        strides instead of a thousand bytes at a time, and the repaint
        no longer holds the audio thread hostage across the vblank
        wait, which was the dropout people hit scrolling during
        playback.

    And the crash is gone. Fast browsing or editing on the instrument
        screen could freeze the machine and reboot it; it turned out to
        be four separate defects stacked -- key repeat running input on
        a background thread, screens rebuilding themselves under the
        control being edited, a stack smash drawing long sample
        filenames, and observer lists mutated mid walk. All four are
        fixed, and the machinery that found them left the input system
        cleaner than it was.

    Settings commit whenever you leave the screen, whatever door you
        use, and stepping the theme repaints the palette live so you
        can shop for one. The mixer's master strip grew its fader. The
        drive, freeze and the per channel phaser and chorus from the
        effect rack are live; the duck, gate and comp controls beside
        them still wait for their processing.

PSPATTERN 0.14.0a

    An effect rack. The delay and reverb move off the mixer's one
        cramped line and onto their own FX screen, left of the mixer on
        the map, with room to grow -- and grow they do. The reverb
        learns to FREEZE: feedback goes lossless and the tail hangs
        forever, so a chord can be caught and held as a pad to play
        over. A DRIVE control saturates the whole wet bus through a
        soft clip, from a warm push to a crushed, dirty space. Both run
        on the Media Engine beside the effects they colour, and both
        work identically with the second core switched off.

    And every channel gets two insert effects of its own: a phaser and
        a chorus, each with its own rate and depth, applied after the
        fader and before the sends so the movement carries into the
        echo and the room. A channel that leaves them at zero pays
        nothing. They are edited on the FX screen through a channel
        selector; the duck, gate, and comp controls drawn beside them
        are wired to the engine but their processing lands in the next
        release -- moving them does not yet change the sound.

    The mixer's master strip grows a fader. The master volume lived
        only on the project screen; now the cursor walks one column
        further right and the same value sits under the master meters,
        where a mix is actually balanced.

    Settings become a screen. config.xml can now be edited on the
        device: a SETTINGS page between PROJECT and GROOVE carries the
        theme (applied live), the font, the audio buffer, the Media
        Engine offload, and the FM engine toggle, each marked when a
        reboot is needed to take. Saving preserves every comment in the
        file. Underneath, the FM engine is hidden from the instrument
        picker by default -- it is the one engine that can outrun the
        DSP budget -- switchable back on in config; instruments already
        saved as FM always load and play. The Media Engine offload
        gained the same runtime switch, on by default.

    The VOX formant bank now runs on the VFPU in the shipping build,
        verified on hardware: a VOX-heavy song dropped about ten
        percent DSP, and high-resonance vowels stay clean.

PSPATTERN 0.13.0a

    A new voice. VOX is a formant engine, and where the others subtract
        from a rich source with one filter, warp a single oscillator, or
        stack sines into metal, it holds several resonant peaks at once
        -- which is what a vowel is. A band-limited glottal source and a
        breath of noise run through a bank of parallel bandpass filters
        tuned to a vowel's formants, and one knob morphs between two
        vowels you pick. It sings: choir pads that breathe, talkbox
        leads, an "ooh" bass. It is also far cheaper than the four
        operator FM it stands beside -- one source and a handful of
        filters, no wavetable to miss in the cache -- so it carries the
        polyphony FM runs out of budget for.

    Underneath, the audio path got cheaper. The mixer was scanning each
        block three times to read its meters and two of those passes
        were redundant; they are gone, and the output is identical to
        the sample. The FM operator's sine was two table reads and two
        held pointers per sample and is now one of each. Neither was a
        guess: a compile-time profiler now attributes render time by
        stage, so the next saving is aimed rather than hoped at.

    And the edges people actually touch. A new synth now arrives with a
        gentle amplitude envelope already on, because nearly every patch
        wants one. Moving up and down through a synth's controls is
        reversible again -- up undoes a down instead of occasionally
        stepping a column sideways -- and the selected control wears a
        clear marker rather than a faint change of shade. Cloning a
        phrase or a chain drops the copy beside its source instead of at
        the lowest free number. And, for anyone who turns it on, a
        looping phrase can follow the edit cursor down the list the way
        the M8 does.

PSPATTERN 0.12.0a

    The send effects moved onto the second core. The PSP has two
        Allegrex processors and until now the audio ran entirely on the
        first. The whole send bank -- the delay and the FDN reverb --
        now runs on the Media Engine, the second core, which had never
        before carried live audio effects inside a music program. The
        main core hands the two send accumulators across and takes the
        finished wet signal back, and the work in between happens
        somewhere it used to have to pay for. This is where the headroom
        in this release comes from.

    Getting there meant teaching that core things it did not have. Its
        floating-point unit has no hardware for denormal numbers, so a
        reverb tail decaying toward silence would trap and freeze the
        machine; the fix flushes those values to zero in the unit's
        control register and in the reverb math. The core also cannot
        see the first core's data cache, so every buffer that crosses
        between them travels through an uncached window and a hand-off
        that drains the write buffer before the first core reads it. A
        wet ring smooths the seam so no block boundary is audible.

    The meters and the scope draw on the GPU. The graphics chip sat idle
        while the mixer channel, the level meters and the oscilloscope
        were painted a pixel at a time on the main core. They are now
        drawn as sprites on the GPU, which is both faster and one more
        thing off the processor that runs the sequencer. The display
        stops lagging when the DSP load is high.

    The master EQ shows numbers. The per-band setting was a row of
        glyphs that meant little at a glance; each band now reads out in
        decibels, coloured by whether it cuts or boosts.

    A note on standby. While the second core is running effects, the
        PSP's sleep switch is held off: the stock standby handler waits
        on the Media Engine in a way a core running our own code cannot
        answer, so closing the lid mid-session is disabled rather than
        risk a hang. This is a deliberate trade for the headroom, on a
        build still under active development.

PSPATTERN 0.11.0a

    The audio DSP moved onto the VFPU. The PSP's vector unit sat idle
        while the mixer strained on the main core; three of the
        heaviest stages now run on it, which is where the headroom for
        this release comes from.

    The reverb is a new engine. The old eight-comb Freeverb bank fought
        the vector unit -- mismatched line lengths, serial chains, and
        a quantisation tail that never fully decayed -- so it was
        replaced with a four-line Feedback Delay Network, whose 4x4
        feedback matrix is a single vtfm4.q instruction. It is a
        denser, cleaner reverb, structurally cheaper than the comb bank
        even before the vector unit, and it runs at half rate the same
        way the old bank did. Reverb load fell from about 19% of a
        block to 15%.

    The master EQ runs on the VFPU. Its ten-band one-pole ladder now
        processes four bands to a lane, capping the cost of a heavy
        sculpt -- the case the difference form made expensive. The
        sound is unchanged: the float ladder tracks the fixed EQ to a
        single 16-bit LSB across every setting.

    The soft clipper is a branchless block, ready for the same
        treatment. It folds the standard-cubic curve's in-range and
        saturated cases into one expression and lands on the identical
        int16 -- no default build's sound moves.

    The display stops freezing under high DSP. The scope and the meters
        and the ability to navigate all live on the main thread, and
        the audio render -- an SDL worker thread -- outranked it, so a
        heavy block starved the UI. The render now round-robins with
        the main thread at an equal priority; the audio buffer's lead
        absorbs the interleaving and the sound is not put at risk.

    The audio thread stopped calling malloc. Every block used to
        allocate a buffer on the way into the pool and free it on the
        way out, taking the heap lock on the one path that must not
        stall. The pool hands blocks back for reuse now; after the
        first lap it touches the heap not at all.
PSPATTERN 0.10.1a

    A ten band master graphic EQ, on the mixer screen. One stage, on
        the summed output ahead of the clipper -- not per instrument.
        The bands are the same complementary one-pole set PSPECTRA
        uses, so flat truly passes the signal through untouched, and
        bands you have not moved cost nothing: bass and treble carved
        together runs two filters, not nine. X+O resets a band, and
        the readout speaks decibels.

    Each mixer strip says what kind of instrument it is carrying --
        sampler, tone, VA, PDX, FM, drum -- so a screen of eight bars
        no longer needs the song page open beside it to know who is
        who.

    The tearing in the mixer bars is fixed. The repaint now waits for
        the vertical blank before the pixels are copied, not before
        they are drawn, which is the difference between waiting for
        the crossing and waiting near it.

    The audio thread no longer touches the heap. Every block used to
        malloc on the way into the buffer pool and free on the way
        out, and the heap lock those calls take is shared with
        everything else that allocates -- so a song load, a memory
        probe or an autosave on the other thread could stall the
        audio callback mid-block. The pool now hands blocks back for
        reuse instead; after the first lap it allocates nothing at
        all. This changes no measurement and removes a class of rare,
        unreproducible ticks.

    Groundwork, inaudible: the send effects are partitioned behind a
        tested hand-over protocol, so the day a second core is proved
        safe to use, the delay and reverb can move onto it as a change
        in one place.

PSPATTERN 0.10.0a

    The tracker ships with headroom now. Every default in the gain
        chain was exactly unity, so the first thing a new user met was
        the ceiling: one voice measured -9.1 dBFS, TWO channels fit,
        and THREE clipped.

    The master fader defaults to 75, which its fourth-power taper makes
        -10.00 dB exactly. Eight channels now land at -1.1 dBFS. Only
        new projects start there -- every project already saved carries
        its own master value and keeps it.

    The sampler was six decibels hot, and it was the engine rather than
        the drums. A full scale sample at stock settings reached -3.04
        dBFS where every synth engine reached -9.1: the sampler's
        DEFAULT sat at full scale while the synth's sat at half. The
        loudest baked drum measured -3.31 dBFS, so two on the same step
        clipped. Four fit now, and the kits themselves are untouched.

    The note-on click is fixed. The channel's declick was armed exactly
        backwards -- set only when the channel was already idle, and
        left clear whenever there was actually a step to smooth. The
        sampler's note-on transient falls from 43.8dB above its own
        steady state to 14.6dB, and an instrument change on a channel
        from a step a third of full scale wide to one thirtieth of it.

    The sampler grew the declick the synth has had for a while, and
        PDX stopped recomputing its phase distortion warp in four
        treads across a note-on.

    Mute, solo and the faders ramp. Every gain change used to be a hard
        step between blocks -- a discontinuity every 5.8ms whenever a
        control moved.

    The meters read in decibels. They were linear in amplitude, which
        was survivable only while everything ran at the ceiling; once
        the headroom arrived a synth at full blast drew a tenth of the
        bar. A voice at -9.1 dBFS draws 71 pixels of 88 now instead of
        30.

    Channel bars are read after the channel's own gain, so the mixer
        controls move them. A bar no longer freezes at its last peak
        when its channel goes quiet, and meter fall is no longer tied
        to frame rate.

    The "sum" figure is now "sat NN%": how much of the moment the
        master sum spent pinned at the top. Zero means the mix fits.
        The old figure claimed to be the bus sum before the fader and
        could never print more than 1.00x however hot the mix was.

    Autosave never runs during playback. It used to hold off for two
        minutes and then write anyway, which meant a Memory Stick write
        -- and an audible dropout -- at the two minute mark, in a music
        program, while you were listening.

    The drum kit is baked once. It was rebuilt on every song load and
        thrown away on every song close, about a megabyte of heap churn
        per load to arrive at the bytes that had just been freed.

PSPATTERN 0.9.0a

    The note-on click, found and fixed. It was never a fade that was
        too short: the channel's declick was armed exactly backwards.

    The channel keeps a measured step corrector -- on a note start it
        takes the jump between the last sample it emitted and the first
        sample of the new block, cancels it, and decays that away over
        about 0.7ms. It was armed by StopInstrument, which returns
        early the moment the outgoing instrument is releasing. Release
        0 has been a 128 sample fade since the earlier declick work, so
        "releasing" is true straight after stopping anything that was
        still sounding.

    So the flag was left CLEAR whenever there was a step to smooth, and
        SET only when the channel was already idle and both sides of
        the join were zero. The correction ran where nothing needed
        correcting and stood down where the click was. It is armed on
        every note start now, which is safe because it measures the
        real jump: a note starting near where the last one ended gets a
        correction of nearly nothing.

    The sampler's note-on transient falls from 43.8dB above its own
        steady state to 14.6dB. An instrument change on a channel falls
        from a step a third of full scale wide to one thirtieth of that.

    The sampler also had no declick of its own, which the synth has had
        for a while. Its new note already faded in from zero; what it
        never handled was the note it replaced, cut dead wherever its
        waveform stood. It measures and decays that step now.

    PDX had a second transient, unrelated. It recomputed its phase
        distortion warp every 32 samples, so a note-on crossed the mod
        envelope in four treads and put a step 12.7% of full scale wide
        at exactly sample 128. At a refresh of 8 that is gone. What is
        left is the mod envelope itself -- the warp starts at full and
        decays, and with the warp amount at zero there is no transient
        at all. That is what phase distortion sounds like.

    The meters mean something now.

    Channel bars were read before the channel's own gain, so nothing
        the mixer could do moved them: turn the gain up and the master
        bar rose while the strip responsible sat still. They are read
        after it now. The master fader is deliberately left out of
        them, because a channel bar answers what a strip is sending,
        not what survives the master.

    A bar froze at its last peak when its channel went quiet, because
        the peaks were only written on blocks that had audio and the
        decay only pulls a bar down when the new peak is lower.

    Meter fall was tied to frame rate again. The elapsed-time helper
        resets its own clock when asked, and the mixer asked twice a
        frame, so the decay always saw about a millisecond and fell one
        pixel per repaint.

    The master fader now acts BEFORE the buses are summed. The
        accumulator saturates once two full scale sources are in it, so
        a fader applied after the sum was attenuating audio that had
        already been flattened -- it changed how loud the damage was
        and removed none of it.

    The "sum" figure on the mixer title strip is now "sat NN%": how
        much of the moment the master sum spent pinned at the top. The
        old figure claimed to be the bus sum before the fader and was
        wrong twice over -- it was read after the sum, so moving the
        fader made it silently include the fader, and the accumulator
        clamps every partial add so it could never print more than
        1.00x however hot the mix was.

    No package ships demo songs any more, in any mode.

PSPATTERN 0.8.3a

    Following an external clock is early now, on purpose.

    0.8.2a locked the two clocks together and that was still not the
        same as the two being heard together. What gets aligned is the
        moment the tracker decides a tick. What anyone hears is that
        tick coming out of the audio buffer 35ms later at the default
        settings, on top of however long the clock byte took to
        arrive. So a perfectly locked song sat perfectly late.

    The loop targets a lead rather than zero phase: the song runs
        early by the size of its own audio buffer, which it can
        measure, so the sound lands on time. MIDISYNCOFFSET in
        config.xml trims the rest -- milliseconds, signed, positive
        plays earlier -- because the leader's own output latency and
        the USB input path are not knowable from in here.

    The lead is held in milliseconds and converted using the current
        tempo, since a tick is shorter at 160 than at 90. Kept in
        ticks it would have gone out by more the faster the song ran.

    Fixed, and it was a fault in 0.8.2a: the loop hunted instead of
        settling. Tick counts are whole numbers and the lead is not --
        35ms at 120bpm is 1.68 ticks -- so the raw phase error could
        never reach zero, and integrating it swung the tempo about
        1.5% for ever. The integral is fed a smoothed error now.
        Averaging recovers what is below the quantiser: the loop
        dithers between the two nearest whole positions and the mean
        of that dither is the true fractional offset.

    The gains were then measured rather than guessed, against how far
        the audio ends up from the leader in milliseconds. A faster
        proportional term wins on that even though the tempo readout
        wobbles more, which is the right trade for a sequencer.

    Fixed: deep clone cleared the L bit after testing the R one, a
        leftover from moving shortcuts off R. The R bit stayed set, so
        the view dropped straight back into clone mode on the way out
        of a deep clone.

    The manual had two CLONING sections that named different buttons.
        Both describe what the program does now.

PSPATTERN 0.8.2a

    Following an external clock now locks to the leader's POSITION,
        not just a guess at its speed.

    What it did before: time the gap between quarter notes, turn that
        into a whole number of beats per minute, smooth it, write it to
        the project tempo. That is an estimate of how fast the leader
        is going and says nothing about where it is, so a song that
        came in at the wrong moment stayed at the wrong moment for
        good. Three things made it worse. Whole beats per minute is a
        0.78% step at 128, which is 62ms of drift across an eight
        second phrase. The timestamps came from a pump that sleeps up
        to 20ms and hands over whatever arrived in a burst. And the
        smoothing added a bar of lag to any real change.

    What it does now: one MIDI clock is exactly one tracker tick, both
        being twenty four to the quarter note, so the leader's clocks
        and our own slices are the same unit and are simply counted
        against each other. The difference between the counts is the
        phase error in ticks, and counting is exact even when the
        timing of the bytes is not -- a burst cannot lose a count.

    A PI loop closes that error. The proportional half pulls a gap out
        quickly, the integral half is what learns the tempo, since a
        standing phase error is precisely what a wrong tempo produces.
        The loop is zeroed on the leader's start byte, the one moment
        the two clocks are known to agree.

    Tempo is a float on the way in now. The sample counts underneath
        always were; only the way in was rounded to whole beats.

    The bpm figure on the song screen is coloured while following: the
        play colour once locked, the mute colour while still chasing.
        A leader that never lets it lock is now something you can see
        rather than only hear.

    tools/run_clocksync_test.sh runs the loop against a simulated
        leader: same tempo, unknown tempo, a song that came in eight
        ticks late, a tempo change mid song, and a leader that stops
        sending.

PSPATTERN 0.8.1a

    Follow mode works at all now, and works the way a piece of MIDI
        gear in external sync works.

    Every realtime byte was being thrown away by the PSP's MIDI input
        driver. onPacket kept USB-MIDI code index 0x8 to 0xE, channel
        voice, on the grounds that the mapping engine had no use for
        realtime and the clock slave "is not wired upstream either".
        It had been wired upstream the whole time: MidiNoteInput
        dispatches F8, FA, FB and FC and acts on all four. Nothing ever
        reached it, so Follow neither took a tempo nor answered a
        transport on hardware.

    On Follow, START no longer starts the song. It arms it, and the
        bottom row reads "waiting for midi start" until the leader's
        start byte arrives. Pressing START again gives up the wait. A
        leader's stop while waiting cancels it too.

    Which is the point of a slave transport: a local start in Follow
        used to begin playing immediately, beside the leader rather
        than with it, and at whatever the leader's tempo happened to
        have nudged the song to.

    Stop stays immediate and stays local. A song you want stopped
        should stop when you say so.

    The waiting line is drawn on every screen, not just the one that
        started it, because the leader can come in while you are
        editing a phrase. It does not blink: only the song and mixer
        screens repaint on a timer, so anywhere else a blink would stop
        on whichever phase it caught.

PSPATTERN 0.8.0a

    The song screen says whether the adapter is really there.

    The mid row read the configured device name out of the config file,
        so it printed PSPMIDI with nothing plugged in. The one line on
        screen that looked like it answered "is my adapter working"
        always said yes.

    It has three answers now, because the two failures need different
        ones: the device name when an adapter is connected, "no cable"
        when the driver is loaded and nothing is answering it, and
        "no drv" in the warning colour when usbmidi.prx never loaded at
        all -- which no cable will fix. It updates while you work, so
        you can plug the adapter in and watch it change.

    pspUsbMidiConnected() had been declared, stubbed and wired to the
        kernel module since the link was written, and nothing had ever
        called it. It is polled a few times a second rather than every
        frame, since it is a syscall and that panel repaints constantly.

    Corrected, and it was wrong in both directions before this: the
        tracker DOES send MIDI clock. Twenty four pulses to the quarter
        note, with start and stop when the transport moves, on by
        default, no setting to enable. The feature list had claimed it
        does not, and the MIDI section had been rewritten to agree.

    MIDISENDSYNC turns it off, for a rig where something else is the
        master and two clocks would fight. That key was documented
        nowhere; it is in the manual, on the site and commented in the
        shipped config.xml now.

    The manual also still described the song screen panel as showing
        DSP load. That moved to the title strip in 0.2.8a and the
        memory bar took its place.

PSPATTERN 0.7.0a

    Two commands, both of them things LSDJ has had for years and this
        did not.

    MAYB aa-b is a step that only sometimes happens. aa is the odds
        out of FF -- 00 never, FF always, 80 about half. A step with
        no MAYB on it always happens, so nothing written before this
        changes.

    In a phrase it decides whether the note plays. A step it skips is
        left alone rather than cut off, so whatever was ringing keeps
        ringing, exactly as an empty step would. A maybe that cut the
        previous note would be a gate, not a maybe.

    In a table it is a hop that only sometimes hops, and b is the row
        to land on -- the same parameter shape HOP already uses, with
        the repeat count replaced by odds. The three command columns
        in a table each walk their own position, so one column can
        wander while the others hold.

    VIBR aabb wobbles the pitch: aa speed, bb depth in sixteenths of a
        semitone, so 10 is one semitone and 02 the smallest wobble
        worth having. 0000 turns it off and puts the pitch back.

    It is centred on the note, so a part with vibrato sits at the same
        average pitch as one without -- the difference between vibrato
        and a detune that wobbles. It also rides on top of whatever
        pitch the step is already playing, so vibrato and an arpeggio
        run at once instead of overwriting each other.

    All three instruments have it. The sampler fills in a Vibrato
        updater that the tracker this is forked from left as a
        commented out stub; the synth multiplies the phase increment;
        MIDI sends it as pitch bend, which means the device's own bend
        range decides how far it goes.

    Fixed: the synth's note-on set its phase increment directly
        instead of going through setVoicePitch, so the voice never
        recorded which note it was playing. Nothing had needed that
        until VIBR 0000 asked for the pitch back and got the previous
        note's.

    The on screen help knows about CHRD, RTGR, RAND and TRSP now. All
        four shipped in 0.5.0a with no help entry, which meant the one
        place you can look a command up on the device did not have
        them.

PSPATTERN 0.6.0a

    Song rows can be bookmarked. SELECT and O marks the row under the
        cursor, SELECT and X clears it, and SELECT with up or down
        jumps to the next mark in that direction. A song is 256 rows
        and the screen shows sixteen, so the intro and the last chorus
        were a long way apart. Marks are saved with the song, and a
        song saved with marks still opens in an older build.

    SELECT does something now. The button was in the enum, commented
        out of the event dispatcher and left out of the PSP mapping
        entirely, so it had never been wired to anything -- a whole
        modifier nobody could press. It is a modifier now, which is
        where the bookmark bindings went rather than into an already
        crowded chord.

    A marked row is drawn in the value colour instead of the row
        colour. The font is a bitmap with no per-glyph table, so a
        marker character is a guess that fails silently; the row number
        was already on screen and already coloured.

    The cue row counts down. A queued channel used to show a blinking
        arrow whose blink rate encoded which boundary it was waiting
        for, which is not something anyone reads mid-set. The same
        single column now shows how many steps are left before the
        channel actually switches, counting down to the change, in the
        play colour to start and the mute colour to stop. More than
        sixteen steps out reads +.

    The count comes from the same two rules the player uses to end a
        phrase and a chain, and is tested against them directly, since
        a countdown that reaches zero a step early is worse on stage
        than no countdown.

    Fixed: an unmapped controller trigger read an uninitialised
        variable and shifted a bit by whatever was on the stack.

PSPATTERN 0.5.0a

    Tables have a transpose column. Sixteen rows of semitones on the
        left of the three command columns, shifting the note the table
        is running against. It is an offset rather than a new note, so
        an arpeggio running underneath arpeggiates around the
        transposed pitch instead of cancelling it, and a row left at
        -- changes nothing.

    Projects written before this load with the column at zero, and
        projects written after it open in an older build with the
        column ignored. Neither direction needs anything from you.

    Four new commands.

    CHRD abcd is a chord: the same four semitone offsets ARPG takes,
        always cycled at tick rate, so there is no ARPS to set as
        well. The channels are monophonic, so this is what a chord can
        be.

    RTGR aabb is the shape of a retrigger: aa is the volume change per
        repeat and bb the pitch change in semitones, both signed. Put
        it in the other command column beside RTRG. Synth only.

    RAND aabb scatters the note it sits beside, aa semitones of pitch
        either way and bb of level. Level only ever goes down, because
        a command that could make a step louder than the one you set
        is a command that clips a mix you had balanced. Synth only.

    TRSP --bb shifts the note by bb semitones and holds it there. This
        is what the table's transpose column emits, and it is useful
        typed into a step on its own.

    Opening a project left tables 08 to 7F holding whatever the last
        project had in them. Only the first eight were being cleared.

    The website has a plain list of everything the program does.

PSPATTERN 0.4.0a

    Four palettes instead of one, picked by a single line in
        config.xml: SUNSET, which is what shipped, ICEBOX, EMBER and
        GRAPHITE. Any individual colour named in the file still wins
        over the theme, so you can take a palette and change one
        thing. If you are updating an older config.xml that spelled
        all thirteen colours out, delete those lines or the theme has
        nothing left to set.

    The instrument screen draws the FM routing. A box per operator,
        laid out by how far it is from the output, with a line from
        each one to whatever it feeds. The box is the level meter too,
        so an operator turned down is visibly an empty box; carriers
        are drawn in the highlight colour and modulators in the
        structure colour. It reads the same table the engine plays
        from, so the picture cannot drift from the sound.

    The phrase screen shows what just fired. A short bar beside every
        row that has a note and has just been passed, brightest on the
        row sounding now and fading over the three behind it. Gaps
        stay dark, runs light up in sequence, and the rhythm you are
        listening to is visible instead of inferred.

    The scope draws both channels, one trace above the other, same
        size. It was the left channel only, so a hard panned part or a
        ping pong delay looked exactly like a mono mix.

    The boot screen has the wordmark on it, for the few seconds the
        sample bank takes to open.

PSPATTERN 0.3.1a

    The DSP figure no longer shoots past 100 while rendering. It was
        not lying about the time, but it was calling it the wrong
        thing: the wav writer's flush happens inside the block being
        measured, because the tap that feeds it sits in the mixer's
        render. A 32KB write to a Memory Stick takes tens of
        milliseconds against a block budget of about six, so the block
        it lands on really did take that long -- and nothing was wrong,
        because the prebuffer covers it and the song plays straight
        through.

    A meter labelled dsp should say how close the DSP is to its
        deadline. Card I/O is not that, and showing it as if it were
        teaches people to ignore the number, which is worse than not
        having one. The writer times its own flush now and that time
        comes back off the total.

    Subtracted, not hidden: if a write ever gets slow enough to outlast
        the prebuffer, that is audible, and it is audible in the usual
        way rather than as a figure nobody trusts.

PSPATTERN 0.3.0a

    The SONG box on the project screen has five rows in it and was
        drawn four rows tall, so repeat sat on the bottom border
        instead of inside the box.

    Starting a render no longer stalls the song. The wav header was
        written as twelve separate calls -- four bytes, two bytes,
        four bytes -- and on a Memory Stick a write is a card
        transaction rather than a buffered nothing. Twelve of them
        landed at the instant the transport started. It is built in
        memory and written once now. The samples already went through
        a 32KB buffer; this is the one write that was still doing it
        the old way.

    Render Stems is gone. Eight files being written at once is 1.4MB
        a second to a card that does not want it in those pieces, and
        it never worked on hardware -- the render came out glitched,
        which is the worst way for a bounce to fail, because it looks
        like it worked until you listen to it. Stereo is unchanged.

    The send effects were rebuilt underneath, with no change to what
        they sound like. Everything the delay and reverb touch is one
        structure now rather than fifteen separate pieces of state,
        and it reads the knob positions as they were when the block
        started rather than reading them live -- so moving the reverb
        size while a block is being processed can no longer change the
        coefficients halfway through it.

    They can also run a block behind, which is off unless you set
        DEFERREDFX in config.xml. That exists for testing and there is
        no reason to turn it on otherwise: it costs a few milliseconds
        of latency on the wet signal and gives nothing back.

    Why any of that was done: the plan was to move the delay and
        reverb onto the PSP's second CPU, the Media Engine, which does
        nothing in a tracker and would have been worth about a third
        of a block. It is not happening. Half of the Media Engine core
        function table is unmapped on the PSP-1000 -- 37 of 75 entries
        against none on the slim and later -- so it would have worked
        on some machines and not others, and it needs a kernel module
        to do it.

    The bench measures the send bus as well as the voices, and
        docs/PERFORMANCE.md has the hardware figures, including the
        five things that were tried and did not work.

PSPATTERN 0.2.9a

    FM costs a sixth less than it did. Four operators at eight voices
        was 85% of one block on hardware and is 71%. Both halves of
        that came from measuring on a PSP; nothing on a build host or
        in an emulator could have shown either.

    The engine was not compute bound and not cache bound. It was
        spilling: 317 of its 547 memory operations were the compiler
        shuffling registers to the stack, because four operators of
        state do not fit in the register file. The operator loop is
        written out as scalars now instead of looping over arrays,
        which also drops three dead feedback tests, op0's routing
        (every algorithm sends it to the output) and op3's incoming
        modulation slot (nothing in any algorithm feeds op3, so it
        read a zero every sample).

    An integer division was running once per sample: the carrier sum
        divided by the carrier count. On this chip div is about
        thirty five cycles and does not pipeline. The count is 1 to 4
        and fixed for the whole render, so it is a reciprocal now.
        Negative sums can land one LSB lower than before.

    The CPU LOAD bench measures the send bus as well as the voices --
        delay, reverb, both, with the step axis reading as channels
        sending rather than voices sounding.

    Hardware figures are in docs/PERFORMANCE.md, including the four
        things that were tried and did not work. The emulator is 1.4
        to 1.8x optimistic on voices and 1.0 to 1.2x on the send bus.

PSPATTERN 0.2.8a

    The song screen has a memory bar where the dsp row was. The dsp
        figure was already on the title strip, so one of the five rows
        that panel has was spent saying something the screen already
        said. The overrun warning moved up there with it, and shows as
        dsp999! in the mute colour.

    Samples live in RAM because streaming them off the Memory Stick is
        not viable, so what is left is what decides whether the next
        one loads, and there was no way to find out until it did not.

    Two changes about where things sit relative to the PSP's 16KB data
        cache. Neither changes any arithmetic.

    The reverb's twenty four comb and allpass lines were twenty four
        separate allocations, so where they landed relative to each
        other was whatever the heap felt like that run. They are one
        block now, cache line aligned with a line of skew between
        them.

    The synth's lookup tables are 8.5KB against that same 16KB cache.
        They live in the PSP's scratchpad now: on chip, not cached,
        not competing with the audio for lines. Set SCRATCHPADTABLES
        to 0 in config.xml to put them back.

    Neither effect can be seen in an emulator, which models no cache.
        Watch the dsp figure on a reverb heavy song on hardware.

    sendfx_test existed and had never been wired up. It runs now, and
        under ASan.

PSPATTERN 0.2.7a

    Note ons are smoother. Three places where a parameter of 0 meant a
        step rather than the shortest fade:

    The sampler had no fade at all at its defaults. Attack 0, decay 0,
        full sustain, release 0 is what every plain sample uses, and
        that combination skipped the envelope entirely and started the
        note at full level in one sample. The step was the size of
        whatever the wav opened on. It fades now and then takes itself
        back out of the per-sample path, so a plain sample still costs
        nothing to play once the note is under way.

    Release 0 cut the voice where it stood, on both the sampler and the
        synth. Where one note is followed by the next, that cut lands
        on the same boundary as the note on, so it is heard as a click
        on the note coming in rather than on the one that ended. Both
        fade now.

    The shortest fade is 2.9ms rather than 1.45ms, and it is one
        constant used by attack, decay and release in both engines, so
        0 means the same thing everywhere. Still far too short to read
        as an attack or a release.

    A release tail is no longer mistaken for a sounding note. It was
        one flag answering two questions, and once every note had a
        tail the answers stopped agreeing: a note after silence glided
        up from the pitch of the note before it, and the FM operator
        envelopes carried over instead of starting again. The
        amplitude envelope still picks up from the tail, which is the
        point of it; nothing that shapes the timbre does.

PSPATTERN 0.2.6a

    R with a direction moves between screens and does nothing else.
        Everything that was held with R and was not navigation is held
        with L now, on every screen, the way the mixer already worked:
        mute, solo, and interpolate.

    The combos those collided with went the other way onto R: start a
        selection, clone, paste, cut, and widen. Nothing there can
        clash with the screen map, because a combo held with X or O
        never reaches the code that reads the map.

    On the mixer, the four settings of the send effects are walked with
        left and right. They sit side by side on one line and always
        did; reaching them took up and down. Up from that line returns
        to the channel strips.

    The version the program announces comes from the same place the
        release is named after. It had drifted to 0.2.0a while the
        builds went out as 0.2.5a.

    DEMO-3, a chiptune arrangement that leans on PDX. Not shipped in
        tester builds, which carry no songs at all.

PSPATTERN 0.2.5a

    The audio block was 128 frames, which gave the whole render 2.9ms
        to finish, 344 times a second. A block that overruns is filled
        with silence by the driver, and a run of those is a buzz at the
        block rate sitting on top of the music rather than a dropout
        anybody would recognise as one. That is the grit, and it is why
        it never appeared in the emulator: the arithmetic is identical
        there, only the time budget is not.
    Default is 256 now. Both the block size and the prebuffer count are
        settable in config.xml as AUDIOBUFFERSIZE and AUDIOPREBUFFER.
        If it is still gritty, try 512 and then 1024. Latency is frames
        times prebuffer over 44100.

    Muted channels no longer render. The instrument was rendered in
        full and the mute applied afterwards, so muting saved only the
        channel strip and soloing saved nothing at all. Soloing one
        channel to find out what was overloading the machine reported
        very nearly the load of the whole song.

    The DSP figure shows the worst block when one overruns, instead of
        an average smoothed over eight blocks and then clamped at 99. A
        machine can glitch steadily while an average sits at twenty.

    The channel strip is one pass instead of three. The high pass, the
        low pass and the fader each walked the whole block separately.

    The send path is 32 bit. Accumulate runs for every sample of every
        sending channel and was widening to 64 bit to multiply by a
        0..255 send. The delay line works in whole samples like the
        comb bank already did.

    PDX saw and square dropped a 64 bit multiply per sample that was
        never needed: the compare against the knee already bounds the
        product under 2^31. PDX is also in the load test now, which it
        was never in.

    The band limiting no longer divides per edge; the reciprocal moved
        to the pitch change.

    Wav writes are batched into 32 KB, which is why stems rendering
        broke up on hardware: eight files at 1.4 MB a second in blocks
        of a few hundred bytes is more than a Memory Stick will take.

PSPATTERN 0.2.4a

    No engine changes. Nothing under sources changed since 0.2.3a, so
        the program itself is identical and a tester who already has
        0.2.3a gains nothing by installing this. It is a version for
        the work around the program.

    A patch parameter the loader does not recognise is now a build
        error.
    InstrumentBank::RestoreContent applies a PARAM only where the name
        matches one of the instrument's variables exactly, and skips
        anything else without a word. Both demo songs had been setting
        "cutoff" and "reso" on every drum since they were written; a
        sample instrument's filter is "filter cut" and "filter res",
        and neither number ever reached the engine. The kit was
        balanced by ear against a patch that was ignoring two of its
        settings.
    The song builder now refuses to write a song carrying a parameter
        the loader would drop, and reads the valid names out of the C++
        rather than keeping a copy of them, so the check cannot drift
        from what it checks.
    The dead parameters are removed rather than renamed. Applying them
        now would be an untested change to two finished songs, and a
        4 kHz lowpass on the kick would take exactly the click it is
        supposed to keep. Removing a setting that never applied cannot
        change what either song sounds like.

    DEMO-2: the breakdown chord breathes.
    Row 5 held one strum a bar and then did nothing with it for four
        bars. A table now steps the volume down and back with the
        cutoff following, looping every twelve ticks, which at six
        ticks to a step is an eighth note, so it lands with the open
        hats. The amplitude envelope across that row moves through
        10.9 dB where it was flat. Not in a tester build, which ships
        no demo songs.

    Sampler measured, not yet fixed.
    The interpolator is healthy: at a ratio of 1.4983 it produces
        -76.1 dB of everything-that-is-not-the-fundamental against
        -76.5 dB for the same job done in floating point, so it is
        within half a decibel of what linear interpolation can do.
    Pitching a sample up is the gap. There is no filter before the
        decimation, so a band limited saw played at 1.4983x puts
        -26.5 dB of energy off the harmonic grid. That is worse than
        the oscillators were before they were band limited, and it is
        the next thing to fix.

PSPATTERN 0.2.3a

    FM: the operator sine is interpolated now.
    Every operator read the nearest of 1024 table entries and threw
        away the other 22 bits of phase. Measured on a bare operator
        with nothing modulating it, everything that was not the
        fundamental sat at -55 dB, which matched a no-interpolation
        reference to a tenth of a decibel.
    It matters more in FM than anywhere else in the synth, because a
        modulator's impurity is multiplied into the carrier's spectrum
        rather than just added to the output. Two operator FM had
        -44 dB of energy sitting between the partials where the Bessel
        spectrum says there should be none.
    Now interpolated against a step table, the way msfa does it, so the
        lookup is a multiply and an add. Bare operator -89 dB, two
        operator FM -77 dB between the partials: 34 dB and 33 dB
        better. Costs 4 points at eight voices of four operator FM,
        47 to 51 per cent of a block.

    FM filter fix, a regression from 0.2.2a.
    That release made the SVF tick twice per sample in the TONE and VAX
        engines and rebuilt the shared coefficient table for the
        doubled rate. The FM engine has the same filter and was missed,
        so it was reading half rate coefficients into a single pass and
        every FM patch's filter sat an octave below where its cutoff
        said it was. All three engines tick twice now.

PSPATTERN 0.2.2a

    The oscillators are band limited now.
    Saw and pulse were naive: they stepped from one sample to the next
        without regard for where inside the sample the edge fell, which
        scatters energy off the harmonics and across the spectrum.
        Measured on a rendered note, energy at frequencies that are not
        in the note ran at -30 dB at A2 and -16 dB at A5, worsening
        3 dB an octave upward. That matched a naive reference exactly,
        which is what confirmed there was no band limiting at all.
    Now a two sample PolyBLEP residual at each discontinuity, on the
        main oscillator, on every member of the unison stack and on the
        sub. Same measurement after: -67 dB at A2 and -32 dB at A5, so
        16 dB better across the range and 37 dB at the bottom.
    The divide only runs inside the edge window, which is twice a
        cycle. Cost at eight voices: tone 17 to 18 per cent of a
        block, vax 25 to 30 with the filter change below.

    The filter is a real filter across its whole range.
    A Chamberlin SVF is only stable while its coefficient stays well
        under 2, which capped the table at 7.6 kHz and then clamped.
        A clamped coefficient is not a corner frequency: measured,
        cutoff 255 with resonance was not a 7.6 kHz lowpass but nearly
        flat to 15 kHz, with the warp near the stability limit standing
        in for an open filter. The top third of the knob did almost
        nothing.
    The SVF now ticks twice per sample, which halves the rate it sees
        and buys the headroom to make the range real. Table extended to
        30 Hz - 12.5 kHz. Measured after: cutoff 255 is -3 dB at
        15 kHz, cutoff 192 at 3520 Hz with -32.9 dB at 10 kHz where it
        used to be -21.2, and no resonant hump.
    Cutoff numbers now mean different frequencies than they did. Every
        setting is somewhat brighter than before, which is the safer
        direction, but existing patches are worth a listen.

    DEMO-2 level 0.29 to 0.26. A PolyBLEP residual overshoots slightly
        at each edge and the filter passes more top, which together
        took the render from no clipped samples to 84. Now 11 in 5.7
        million, all of them transient peaks in the four full
        arrangement rows.

PSPATTERN 0.2.1a

    Rendering to wav did not stop when the song stopped.
    Tearing the render down lived in the song screen's START handler, so
        it only ran when somebody pressed START a second time. A song
        that reaches its end with repeat on "once" stops on its own and
        never went through it, so the screen went on saying it was
        rendering over a file the mixer had already closed.
    Worse, the mixer stayed in stereo render mode, so the next START
        opened a fresh writer on the same path. Opening truncates, so a
        finished render was replaced by however much of the new take had
        been written. A 65 second wav came back as 0.78 seconds.
    Now handled where the player's stop event arrives, so it works from
        any screen, and it forces a redraw: a notification is painted
        into the character grid, and once playback stops nothing repaints
        the row it sits on.

    Changing an instrument's type asked "settings are lost" on
        instruments that had no settings.
    The prompt asked IsEmpty, which is the question the save code asks.
        Synth and midi instruments answer "never empty" there because
        they always have to be written to the file, so both always
        warned, including a patch straight out of the box. New projects
        assign a drum to the first instruments automatically, so those
        warned too.
    There is now a separate question for it: has anything been changed
        from the values a new instrument of this type starts with. The
        sample a new project assigns for you and the midi channel the
        bank stamps in by index are not counted, because neither was
        chosen by anybody.

    DEMO-2, a second demo song. Melody led rather than arpeggio led,
        with one arp channel instead of three, and a chord channel that
        runs the same ARPG command at a slower ARPS so it strums instead
        of trilling. Peak -0.09 dBFS, no clipped samples.

PSPATTERN 0.2.0a

    Engine fixes:
    A note that followed another note on the same channel was thrown
        away one audio block after it started. PlayerChannel set its
        releasing_ flag when stopping the old note and nothing cleared
        it, so the next Render read it as "the release finished" and
        dropped the instrument. Every note after the first on any patch
        with a release, cut about 20ms in.
    Note-on declick used the wrong correction. It added the previous
        sample instead of measuring the step, which for a square left
        nearly a full scale jump. Measured at 18.4% of full scale on a
        square, 16.6% on a pulse; now 0.00% on both.
    The declick bound could not cancel the step it measured. Capped at
        half full scale while a loud square swings 57%.
    Songs no longer loop by default.
    The low battery warning inverted once per repaint on a panel that
        repaints every frame, so it strobed instead of blinking. Now on
        the wall clock at 2Hz, with hysteresis on the threshold.
    The project selector listed the program's own samplelib folder.

    Additions:
    Velocity column in the phrase screen, reading 00..99 rather than
        hex. Empty means full, so older projects are unchanged.
    Hard sync and ring modulation on the VAX engine.
    ARPS: arp speed, in ticks per arpeggio position. Default 1 is the
        classic every-tick trill; higher values slow the figure until
        each note in it can be heard as a pitch.
    MANUAL.txt.
    Channel faders are written by the song generators, so a demo can
        carry its own mix balance.

    Interface:
    Instrument values read as decibels, per cent and L/CTR/R instead of
        raw hex where the raw number meant nothing.
    Four strings named buttons the PSP does not have.
    The boot and browser screens named the upstream project.


    Migrate W32 to SDL2, removing need for building in vs2008

